{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JRNp6GfgqSUML4dtgy2VJs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# GUI: translating the TUI to a native desktop application\n2\t\n3\tExploration notes (2026-08-27). Verdict: **REVISIT** — the direction is plausible,\n4\tbut the question as phrased (\"translate our TUI to a nice GUI\") hides a fork that\n5\tdecides the size of everything, and the foundations the GUI would need to be\n6\t*nice* on top of don't exist yet. This document records the fork, what already\n7\tworks, the genuinely new pieces, and the recommended path.\n8\t\n9\t## Context: same week as the web-interface exploration\n10\t\n11\tA sibling question was explored the same day — `docs/web-interface.md`: running\n12\tmanigot on a server with a browser control plane (verdict REVISIT, sequenced\n13\tbehind roadmap items #4 and #5). A native GUI is the *same bet on a different\n14\tsurface*: it answers the same underlying question (\"watch and control jobs\n15\twithout sitting in a terminal\") locally instead of remotely. The two share the\n16\tsame fork and the same missing foundations. The web doc's own caution applies\n17\there verbatim: don't build the same bet on a second surface at the same time.\n18\tThe roadmap (item 6, in-TUI embedded terminal) is the same embedded-terminal bet\n19\ton a *third* surface. Only one of these should be built, on the surface the\n20\tproduct actually commits to.\n21\t\n22\t## The idea\n23\t\n24\tReplace the terminal-bound `mg tui` with a native desktop GUI on Linux (no other\n25\tplatforms for now — consistent with the product's existing Linux-first posture\n26\tand its total lack of a Windows story). A window showing projects, their jobs,\n27\tand job state — with the ability to create jobs, view/edit the four files,\n28\treview diffs, launch agents, watch runs live, and run the lifecycle\n29\t(done / delete / push / merge).\n30\t\n31\t## What already works (the good news)\n32\t\n33\tMost of the heavy machinery is already UI-independent — the TUI calls it\n34\tin-process, and a GUI process can call the same functions in-process:\n35\t\n36\t- **`internal/job`** — `CreateJob`, `FinishJob`, `DeleteJob`, `Discover`,\n37\t  `ReadJDIStatus` are pure functions over a project root. No TTY involved.\n38\t- **`internal/git`** — worktree create/remove, squash merge, push, diff,\n39\t  commits. All host-side, all callable.\n40\t- **`mg jdi`** — already runs fully unattended via the `--print` path, writes a\n41\t  pollable status sidecar (`.manigot/jdi-status/`), handles\n42\t  `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` markers, and pushes ntfy notifications. Exactly the state\n43\t  model a GUI would render.\n44\t- **The state model of the TUI itself** — `mg tui` is already a long-lived\n45\t  process with refresh/polling/timer ticks (ctrl+r, the jdi badge, the spinner).\n46\t  A GUI process is the same shape. This is the 70%-there part.\n47\t- **Linux-only scoping** — fine as a product decision; no Windows story exists\n48\t  at all, and the terminal-candidate table already proves Linux-first.\n49\t\n50\t## The fork that decides everything\n51\t\n52\t**Is the GUI a control plane for background runs, or a home for interactive\n53\tagent sessions?**\n54\t\n55\t- **Control plane (recommended for v1):** browse jobs, view/edit the four\n56\t  files, launch `mg jdi` / one-shot `--print` agent runs in the background,\n57\t  watch them live, review the diff, answer `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` markers, and run\n58\t  the lifecycle (done / delete / push / merge). Reuses everything above.\n59\t- **Interactive agent sessions in the GUI:** embedded terminals so you can chat\n60\t  with an agent from the window. This is the roadmap's item 6 bet (in-TUI\n61\t  embedded terminal — \"biggest bet, last, smallest slice first\"), transplanted\n62\t  onto a second surface. This is the expensive one, and it is already being\n63\t  sized in the roadmap for the TUI.\n64\t\n65\tThe trap is in the word \"translate\". Today every interactive agent launch spawns\n66\ta tmux pane or a terminal window (`internal/launch`) — the TUI's replace-policy\n67\tpane, the gnome-terminal/ptyxis/x-terminal-emulator/konsole/xterm chain, tig.\n68\tA GUI cannot do tmux panes. So a GUI that *replaces* the TUI inherits the\n69\tinteractive question whether it wants it or not. Either it is a control plane\n70\tthat *complements* the TUI (interactive sessions stay in the terminal), or it is\n71\tthe PTY bet. There is no middle, and the choice decides the size of the whole\n72\tthing.\n73\t\n74\t## User perspective\n75\t\n76\tThe TUI's user is a developer who already lives in the terminal. The honest\n77\tcandidates for what a GUI buys them:\n78\t\n79\t- A glanceable, always-open surface for run status without being inside tmux.\n80\t- Rich rendering the terminal can't do well: side-by-side diffs, markdown, real\n81\t  text selection/copy.\n82\t- Native notifications instead of a terminal bell / ntfy on the phone.\n83\t- Discoverability (menus instead of keybindings).\n84\t\n85\tBut the product's own trajectory cuts against the premise: the autonomy story\n86\t(`mg jdi`, headless, the away digest) exists precisely so the human's job is\n87\t*review and decide*, not drive. The roadmap's whole arc makes the terminal matter\n88\t*less*, not more. The cheap version of the \"check on runs without being in a\n89\tterminal\" need already exists — `mg jdi` + ntfy from an always-on box, answered\n90\tfrom a phone (the web doc's \"validate the cheap version first\"). A GUI is a\n91\tjustified answer only if the need that survives that test is *local* and\n92\t*glanceable*; if it is *remote*, the web exploration from the same day is the\n93\tbetter path. Don't build both.\n94\t\n95\t## Scope assessment — the genuinely new pieces\n96\t\n97\tThe UI itself — list/detail/tabs/diff/forms — is a port of existing information\n98\tdesign. The real new work:\n99\t\n100\t1. **Run supervision with streamed logs.** Today's interactive launches spawn\n101\t   windows; a GUI-launched agent must be a detached process with captured output\n102\t   the UI watches live. The status sidecars give you *stage*, not what the agent\n103\t   is *doing*. This is roadmap item 5 (event-streaming) — currently not built.\n104\t   This is the same gap the web doc names, and the biggest single piece.\n105\t2. **Live updates.** \"Nice\" in a GUI means live. Polling the coarse sidecars\n106\t   (what the TUI does on ctrl+r today) is exactly the non-dynamic experience to\n107\t   avoid. \"Dynamic\" should mean the event stream made visible — not 40 widgets.\n108\t3. **Editing.** The TUI suspends into `$EDITOR`; a GUI can't. Embedded markdown\n109\t   edit, or \"open in editor and reload on return\" — small either way, but a\n110\t   decision.\n111\t4. **Diff rendering.** The TUI renders `git diff --stat` as plain text. A\n112\t   side-by-side diff is the single most legitimate \"nice GUI\" win — and a real\n113\t   component of its own.\n114\t5. **Packaging / environment.** A GUI adds a display-server dependency\n115\t   (X11/Wayland) to a product that is otherwise headless-capable — fine for the\n116\t   target user, but a new environment assumption. Desktop entry, icon, updates:\n117\t   new surface the product has never had. Whatever the toolkit, the \"one Go\n118\t   binary\" ethos should be preserved as much as possible.\n119\t\n120\t## What is NOT the blocker\n121\t\n122\t- **The state model.** `mg tui` is already a long-lived process with\n123\t  refresh/polling/timers calling `internal/job` and `internal/git` in-process.\n124\t  A GUI process does the same thing.\n125\t- **Linux-only.** Consistent with the product's existing posture. The one caveat\n126\t  is the display-server dependency above, not the OS choice.\n127\t\n128\t## Concerns\n129\t\n130\t1. **Three surfaces, one bet.** The web doc says don't build the interactive\n131\t   terminal slice on a second surface while it is the roadmap's item 6. A GUI\n132\t   risks the same collapse: the embedded-terminal slice is the identical bet and\n133\t   should be built once, on the surface the product commits to.\n134\t2. **Port, don't re-imagine.** \"Boring in the right places\" is a product\n135\t   property, not an accident. A GUI must port the TUI's information design — id /\n136\t   status / stage / type / date / title, the four files, the diff tab, the same\n137\t   actions — not reinvent what a dashboard should be. This is exactly where\n138\t   \"nice\" goes wrong.\n139\t3. **Foundation sequencing.** A GUI built on today's polling is a worse TUI with\n140\t   more pixels. Built on items #4/#5 it is a genuinely better surface. The\n141\t   difference is the sequence, not the toolkit.\n142\t4. **One-binary ethos.** The web doc calls embedding the UI into the Go binary\n143\t   \"very on-brand\" for a product that reduced itself to one binary on purpose.\n144\t   Packaging cost should be counted up front, not discovered.\n145\t\n146\t## Recommendation\n147\t\n148\t1. **Decide the fork and the relationship to the TUI** — control plane vs.\n149\t   interactive, complement vs. replace. Stake the answer before any code.\n150\t2. **Validate the cheap version first** (same as the web doc): the away digest —\n151\t   `mg jdi` + ntfy on an always-on box, answered from a phone — may cover a large\n152\t   share of the \"check on runs without being in a terminal\" need at near-zero\n153\t   cost. If the need that survives is *local* and *glanceable*, a GUI is\n154\t   justified; if *remote*, the web exploration is the better path. Not both.\n155\t3. **Land roadmap items #4 (headless/queue) and #5 (event-streaming) once**,\n156\t   against the run.log consumer the roadmap names, and let the GUI attach to the\n157\t   event stream afterward.\n158\t4. **Only then** scope the surface job: persistent process, streamed-run\n159\t   supervision, info-design port, editor story, native notifications, diff view.\n160\t   Each is cleanly separable.\n161\t\n162\tThe short version: translating the TUI is the easy 20%. The run-supervision +\n163\tevent-stream foundation is the real work, it is shared with the web direction\n164\texplored the same day, and the fork — control plane or embedded terminals —\n165\tdecides whether this is three clean jobs or one unbounded bet. Decide the fork\n166\tfirst, and don't build two of these surfaces at once."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"561e42e9-f91a-46b3-ab07-d4e54ed01e9c","uuid":"e36de3d5-697f-41f0-b963-80cbe92ed9f6","timestamp":"2026-08-29T12:59:58.422Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/gui.md","content":"# GUI: translating the TUI to a native desktop application\n\nExploration notes (2026-08-27). Verdict: **REVISIT** — the direction is plausible,\nbut the question as phrased (\"translate our TUI to a nice GUI\") hides a fork that\ndecides the size of everything, and the foundations the GUI would need to be\n*nice* on top of don't exist yet. This document records the fork, what already\nworks, the genuinely new pieces, and the recommended path.\n\n## Context: same week as the web-interface exploration\n\nA sibling question was explored the same day — `docs/web-interface.md`: running\nmanigot on a server with a browser control plane (verdict REVISIT, sequenced\nbehind roadmap items #4 and #5). A native GUI is the *same bet on a different\nsurface*: it answers the same underlying question (\"watch and control jobs\nwithout sitting in a terminal\") locally instead of remotely. The two share the\nsame fork and the same missing foundations. The web doc's own caution applies\nhere verbatim: don't build the same bet on a second surface at the same time.\nThe roadmap (item 6, in-TUI embedded terminal) is the same embedded-terminal bet\non a *third* surface. Only one of these should be built, on the surface the\nproduct actually commits to.\n\n## The idea\n\nReplace the terminal-bound `mg tui` with a native desktop GUI on Linux (no other\nplatforms for now — consistent with the product's existing Linux-first posture\nand its total lack of a Windows story). A window showing projects, their jobs,\nand job state — with the ability to create jobs, view/edit the four files,\nreview diffs, launch agents, watch runs live, and run the lifecycle\n(done / delete / push / merge).\n\n## What already works (the good news)\n\nMost of the heavy machinery is already UI-independent — the TUI calls it\nin-process, and a GUI process can call the same functions in-process:\n\n- **`internal/job`** — `CreateJob`, `FinishJob`, `DeleteJob`, `Discover`,\n  `ReadJDIStatus` are pure functions over a project root. No TTY involved.\n- **`internal/git`** — worktree create/remove, squash merge, push, diff,\n  commits. All host-side, all callable.\n- **`mg jdi`** — already runs fully unattended via the `--print` path, writes a\n  pollable status sidecar (`.manigot/jdi-status/`), handles\n  `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` markers, and pushes ntfy notifications. Exactly the state\n  model a GUI would render.\n- **The state model of the TUI itself** — `mg tui` is already a long-lived\n  process with refresh/polling/timer ticks (ctrl+r, the jdi badge, the spinner).\n  A GUI process is the same shape. This is the 70%-there part.\n- **Linux-only scoping** — fine as a product decision; no Windows story exists\n  at all, and the terminal-candidate table already proves Linux-first.\n\n## The fork that decides everything\n\n**Is the GUI a control plane for background runs, or a home for interactive\nagent sessions?**\n\n- **Control plane (recommended for v1):** browse jobs, view/edit the four\n  files, launch `mg jdi` / one-shot `--print` agent runs in the background,\n  watch them live, review the diff, answer `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` markers, and run\n  the lifecycle (done / delete / push / merge). Reuses everything above.\n- **Interactive agent sessions in the GUI:** embedded terminals so you can chat\n  with an agent from the window. This is the roadmap's item 6 bet (in-TUI\n  embedded terminal — \"biggest bet, last, smallest slice first\"), transplanted\n  onto a second surface. This is the expensive one, and it is already being\n  sized in the roadmap for the TUI.\n\nThe trap is in the word \"translate\". Today every interactive agent launch spawns\na tmux pane or a terminal window (`internal/launch`) — the TUI's replace-policy\npane, the gnome-terminal/ptyxis/x-terminal-emulator/konsole/xterm chain, tig.\nA GUI cannot do tmux panes. So a GUI that *replaces* the TUI inherits the\ninteractive question whether it wants it or not. Either it is a control plane\nthat *complements* the TUI (interactive sessions stay in the terminal), or it is\nthe PTY bet. There is no middle, and the choice decides the size of the whole\nthing.\n\n## User perspective\n\nThe TUI's user is a developer who already lives in the terminal. The honest\ncandidates for what a GUI buys them:\n\n- A glanceable, always-open surface for run status without being inside tmux.\n- Rich rendering the terminal can't do well: side-by-side diffs, markdown, real\n  text selection/copy.\n- Native notifications instead of a terminal bell / ntfy on the phone.\n- Discoverability (menus instead of keybindings).\n\nBut the product's own trajectory cuts against the premise: the autonomy story\n(`mg jdi`, headless, the away digest) exists precisely so the human's job is\n*review and decide*, not drive. The roadmap's whole arc makes the terminal matter\n*less*, not more. The cheap version of the \"check on runs without being in a\nterminal\" need already exists — `mg jdi` + ntfy from an always-on box, answered\nfrom a phone (the web doc's \"validate the cheap version first\"). A GUI is a\njustified answer only if the need that survives that test is *local* and\n*glanceable*; if it is *remote*, the web exploration from the same day is the\nbetter path. Don't build both.\n\n## Scope assessment — the genuinely new pieces\n\nThe UI itself — list/detail/tabs/diff/forms — is a port of existing information\ndesign. The real new work:\n\n1. **Run supervision with streamed logs.** Today's interactive launches spawn\n   windows; a GUI-launched agent must be a detached process with captured output\n   the UI watches live. The status sidecars give you *stage*, not what the agent\n   is *doing*. This is roadmap item 5 (event-streaming) — currently not built.\n   This is the same gap the web doc names, and the biggest single piece.\n2. **Live updates.** \"Nice\" in a GUI means live. Polling the coarse sidecars\n   (what the TUI does on ctrl+r today) is exactly the non-dynamic experience to\n   avoid. \"Dynamic\" should mean the event stream made visible — not 40 widgets.\n3. **Editing.** The TUI suspends into `$EDITOR`; a GUI can't. Embedded markdown\n   edit, or \"open in editor and reload on return\" — small either way, but a\n   decision.\n4. **Diff rendering.** The TUI renders `git diff --stat` as plain text. A\n   side-by-side diff is the single most legitimate \"nice GUI\" win — and a real\n   component of its own.\n5. **Packaging / environment.** A GUI adds a display-server dependency\n   (X11/Wayland) to a product that is otherwise headless-capable — fine for the\n   target user, but a new environment assumption. Desktop entry, icon, updates:\n   new surface the product has never had. Whatever the toolkit, the \"one Go\n   binary\" ethos should be preserved as much as possible.\n\n## What is NOT the blocker\n\n- **The state model.** `mg tui` is already a long-lived process with\n  refresh/polling/timers calling `internal/job` and `internal/git` in-process.\n  A GUI process does the same thing.\n- **Linux-only.** Consistent with the product's existing posture. The one caveat\n  is the display-server dependency above, not the OS choice.\n\n## Concerns\n\n1. **Three surfaces, one bet.** The web doc says don't build the interactive\n   terminal slice on a second surface while it is the roadmap's item 6. A GUI\n   risks the same collapse: the embedded-terminal slice is the identical bet and\n   should be built once, on the surface the product commits to.\n2. **Port, don't re-imagine.** \"Boring in the right places\" is a product\n   property, not an accident. A GUI must port the TUI's information design — id /\n   status / stage / type / date / title, the four files, the diff tab, the same\n   actions — not reinvent what a dashboard should be. This is exactly where\n   \"nice\" goes wrong.\n3. **Foundation sequencing.** A GUI built on today's polling is a worse TUI with\n   more pixels. Built on items #4/#5 it is a genuinely better surface. The\n   difference is the sequence, not the toolkit.\n4. **One-binary ethos.** The web doc calls embedding the UI into the Go binary\n   \"very on-brand\" for a product that reduced itself to one binary on purpose.\n   Packaging cost should be counted up front, not discovered.\n\n## Recommendation\n\n1. **Decide the fork and the relationship to the TUI** — control plane vs.\n   interactive, complement vs. replace. Stake the answer before any code.\n2. **Validate the cheap version first** (same as the web doc): the away digest —\n   `mg jdi` + ntfy on an always-on box, answered from a phone — may cover a large\n   share of the \"check on runs without being in a terminal\" need at near-zero\n   cost. If the need that survives is *local* and *glanceable*, a GUI is\n   justified; if *remote*, the web exploration is the better path. Not both.\n3. **Land roadmap items #4 (headless/queue) and #5 (event-streaming) once**,\n   against the run.log consumer the roadmap names, and let the GUI attach to the\n   event stream afterward.\n4. **Only then** scope the surface job: persistent process, streamed-run\n   supervision, info-design port, editor story, native notifications, diff view.\n   Each is cleanly separable.\n\nThe short version: translating the TUI is the easy 20%. The run-supervision +\nevent-stream foundation is the real work, it is shared with the web direction\nexplored the same day, and the fork — control plane or embedded terminals —\ndecides whether this is three clean jobs or one unbounded bet. Decide the fork\nfirst, and don't build two of these surfaces at once.","numLines":166,"startLine":1,"totalLines":166}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CeX5ezdiQaDt9QcDPAnmX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014pvX5wNFQrXashixyULHs1","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/docs/web-interface.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2846,"cache_read_input_tokens":91020,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2846},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"561e42e9-f91a-46b3-ab07-d4e54ed01e9c","uuid":"c07fc32b-bded-4c26-8dc6-3014096c1dac","timestamp":"2026-08-29T12:59:58.690Z","request_id":"req_011CeX5ezASR6JrnKwBbMqDb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014pvX5wNFQrXashixyULHs1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Web interface: running manigot on a server with a browser control plane\n2\t\n3\tExploration notes (2026-08-27). Verdict: **REVISIT** — the direction is right and\n4\tthe product is already 70% server-shaped, but the scope as originally phrased\n5\t(\"command everything… create jobs, delete, merge, pull, whatever\") is a vision,\n6\tnot a buildable unit, and one fork decides everything. This document records the\n7\tfindings, the fork, the scope, and the recommended path.\n8\t\n9\t## Decision recorded (2026-08-27)\n10\t\n11\tA sibling idea was evaluated the same day and rejected: **external ticketing\n12\tintegration (Linear / Gitea)** — syncing manigot's job state into a third-party\n13\tissue tracker. It answers the *same* question this document answers (remote\n14\tvisibility/control of jobs away from the terminal), and the web control plane\n15\treplaces it outright: it renders the real state (four files, diff, run logs)\n16\tnatively and can act on it (launch, merge, delete), whereas a ticketing sync is\n17\ta lossy, view-only shadow that adds a second source of truth. It only re-enters\n18\tconsideration as a later *consumer* of the event stream (post status/comments to\n19\ta team's board) if a team-need appears — never as a parallel control surface.\n20\tThe one piece worth building independently of this decision is the git-forge\n21\tstep (push job branch + open/merge PR on `mg done`) — the web UI needs it too,\n22\tand it is small.\n23\t\n24\t## The idea\n25\t\n26\tRun manigot on an always-on server instead of a personal workstation, and replace\n27\tthe TUI with a secured web interface that talks to a server listener to control\n28\tit. A Svelte UI showing projects, their jobs, and job state — with the ability to\n29\tcreate jobs, delete, merge, pull, launch agents, and watch runs — replacing the\n30\tterminal-bound `mg tui`.\n31\t\n32\t## What already works on a server (the good news)\n33\t\n34\tThe heavy machinery is mostly terminal-independent already:\n35\t\n36\t- **`internal/job`** — `CreateJob`, `FinishJob`, `DeleteJob`, `Discover`,\n37\t  `ReadJDIStatus` are pure functions over a project root. The TUI calls them\n38\t  in-process; an HTTP handler can call the same functions in-process. No\n39\t  subprocess mg, no TTY.\n40\t- **`internal/git`** — worktree create/remove, squash merge, push, diff, commits.\n41\t  All host-side, all callable.\n42\t- **`mg jdi`** — already runs fully unattended via the `--print` path, writes a\n43\t  pollable status sidecar (`.manigot/jdi-status/`), handles `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:`\n44\t  markers, and pushes ntfy notifications. This is exactly the state model a web\n45\t  UI would render — the TUI already polls it; the web UI would poll/stream the\n46\t  same files.\n47\t- **The sweep-commit** — every job-worktree session ends committed by the host.\n48\t  This is what makes headless server operation safe in the first place.\n49\t- **Docker isolation** — the agent containment story is the product's core\n50\t  safety property and works identically on a server. The daemon does not touch\n51\t  it.\n52\t\n53\tThe genuinely new pieces:\n54\t\n55\t1. **A long-running daemon** — today everything is a one-shot CLI that discovers\n56\t   its project from `$PWD`. A server is a new process (`mg serve` or similar)\n57\t   that holds a registry of project roots. **Multi-project is a new concept for\n58\t   manigot** — nothing in the product today knows about more than one project\n59\t   per invocation.\n60\t2. **The HTTP API + auth surface** — today the trust boundary is the user's own\n61\t   terminal; nothing authenticates anything. A server daemon is a remote attack\n62\t   surface holding subscription credentials and docker access. This is the part\n63\t   with real teeth (see Security).\n64\t3. **Background run supervision with streamed output** — today's interactive\n65\t   agent launches spawn terminal windows/tmux panes, which is meaningless on a\n66\t   server. Web-launched agents need to be detached processes with captured logs\n67\t   the UI can watch live.\n68\t4. **The Svelte app itself.**\n69\t5. **\"Pull\"** — there is no host-side fetch/pull in manigot today (only\n70\t   push-to-origin and merge-default-branch). For a server, `mg pull` (fetch +\n71\t   fast-forward the base branch, refuse-or-report on divergence rather than\n72\t   merging blindly) is a small, genuinely useful addition — locally too.\n73\t\n74\t## The fork that decides everything\n75\t\n76\t**Is the web UI a control plane for background runs, or a remote terminal for\n77\tinteractive sessions?**\n78\t\n79\t- **Control plane (recommended for v1):** launch `mg jdi` per job, launch\n80\t  one-shot agent runs via the `--print` path, watch their logs live, review the\n81\t  four files, edit `brief.md`, merge (`done`), delete, push, answer\n82\t  `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` markers. This is the product's own trajectory — the\n83\t  autonomy machinery exists precisely so the human's job is *review and decide*,\n84\t  not drive. It reuses everything above.\n85\t- **Interactive browser terminals** (xterm.js + a PTY bridge so you can drop\n86\t  into a live Claude Code chat from the browser): much heavier, and arguably\n87\t  wrong for a server — the human usually isn't at the server, and an interactive\n88\t  session on a machine other people can reach raises \"who is driving this\"\n89\t  questions. The roadmap already has \"in-TUI embedded terminal\" as its biggest,\n90\t  last bet; building the same bet on a second surface at the same time is how\n91\t  scope explodes.\n92\t\n93\tDecide that fork first, and you've decided the size of the whole thing.\n94\t\n95\t## User perspective\n96\t\n97\tFor the person this actually serves — a developer who wants manigot's work\n98\trunning on a box that's always on, reachable from anywhere — the control-plane\n99\tversion is a genuine unlock: create a job from the browser, fire jdi at it, get\n100\tan ntfy ping when it needs a human, open the verdict from your phone, approve\n101\tand merge. The *interactive* version is a different product aimed at a different\n102\tneed (remote pair-driving an agent), and it's the expensive one.\n103\t\n104\tA caution on the \"sleek, dynamic Svelte interface\": the roadmap's current state\n105\tis praised as \"boring in the right places,\" and that is a product property, not\n106\tan accident. The web UI should be a **port of the TUI's information design** —\n107\tid / status / stage / type / date / title, the four files, the diff tab, the\n108\tsame actions — not a re-imagination of what a dashboard should be. \"Dynamic\"\n109\tshould mean *live updates*, which comes from the event stream, not from adding\n110\t40 controls. If the interface exists at all, it must be as disciplined as the\n111\tTUI.\n112\t\n113\t## Scope assessment\n114\t\n115\tWhat's missing before this is a brief:\n116\t\n117\t- **Single user or team?** One bearer token vs. real accounts. This changes the\n118\t  auth design completely. (Recommendation: single-user/small-team token auth is\n119\t  the right v1; user accounts are a different product.)\n120\t- **Internet-exposed or LAN/VPN?** Determines how much hardening is\n121\t  non-negotiable vs. nice-to-have.\n122\t- **Project registry:** where do the projects come from? A config file of roots\n123\t  is the obvious v1 (a scan-dir is a worse idea — you don't want a daemon\n124\t  auto-adopting repos it finds).\n125\t- **\"Pull\" semantics** need pinning down (fetch + fast-forward, report-don't-\n126\t  merge on divergence, refuse on dirty tree).\n127\t- **Sequencing against the roadmap.** This is the one that matters most: the web\n128\t  UI's core value depends on roadmap items **#4 (headless/cron — queue,\n129\t  non-terminal trigger, away digest)** and **#5 (event-streaming)**. The\n130\t  \"sleek, dynamic\" part *is* the event stream made visible; polling the coarse\n131\t  sidecars is exactly the non-dynamic experience to avoid. The roadmap's own\n132\t  caution applies here as much as it did for the TUI: don't let the web UI's\n133\t  requirements design the event format. So the honest path is: **#4 → #5 → web\n134\t  UI**, with the web UI as the event stream's real consumer — which is a *better*\n135\t  first consumer than the richer run.log the roadmap currently names.\n136\t\n137\t## Security\n138\t\n139\tThe daemon is a new trust boundary with root-adjacent power: it drives docker\n140\tand git and holds subscription credentials. A compromised HTTP endpoint means an\n141\tattacker running agents on the paid subscription and committing to the repos.\n142\tNon-negotiable hardening:\n143\t\n144\t- **TLS** — in-binary or, saner, behind a reverse proxy (Caddy/nginx).\n145\t- **Bearer-token auth** — over TLS only.\n146\t- **Zero path inputs from the client** — every operation resolved against the\n147\t  registered project roots; no job IDs or paths taken from the URL and trusted.\n148\t- **Credentials never returned** by any API or rendered in the UI. The `.env`\n149\t  holds subscription credentials; the daemon must never expose them.\n150\t- **Audit trail** — a remote/shared box changes the accountability picture; the\n151\t  local TUI never needed \"who ran what,\" the server does.\n152\t- **Concurrency** — today every operation is naturally serialized (one CLI\n153\t  process, user-driven). A daemon gets simultaneous web requests plus\n154\t  long-running launches. `internal/git` has no locking; two concurrent\n155\t  `done`/`delete` on the same job would race. The daemon needs per-project (or\n156\t  per-job) serialization of mutating operations.\n157\t- **Cost** — a server makes firing runs easy, and unattended agent runs burn\n158\t  subscription quota. Surface run activity in the UI; keep ntfy as the \"away\n159\t  digest\" rather than building new notification machinery.\n160\t\n161\t## Concerns\n162\t\n163\t1. **The daemon's power** — see Security. This is the part with real teeth.\n164\t2. **Concurrency** — no locking in `internal/git`; the daemon must serialize\n165\t   mutating operations per job.\n166\t3. **Cost** — unattended runs on a server burn subscription quota.\n167\t4. **Scope creep** — \"and pull, whatever\" is how a control-plane job becomes a\n168\t   remote-desktop job. The interactive-terminal slice must be explicitly parked\n169\t   (it's the roadmap's item #6 bet, one surface at a time).\n170\t\n171\t## Recommendation\n172\t\n173\t> **Superseded (2026-08-27):** the \"Job one: the daemon\" slice below — `mg serve`\n174\t> + project registry + bearer-token auth + read-only API — is now **DONE**, as\n175\t> the listener job (see\n176\t> [`docs/jobs/wood_listener-architecture/brief.md`](jobs/wood_listener-architecture/brief.md),\n177\t> which resolves the web-vs-GUI fork and supersedes this recommendation; no need\n178\t> to re-litigate). This document remains the record of the exploration and the\n179\t> later jobs (mutating API + run supervision, then one frontend) still sequence\n180\t> as described.\n181\t\n182\tProceed, in this order:\n183\t\n184\t1. **Decide the fork** — control plane for background runs, no interactive\n185\t   browser terminals in v1. Stake the product on that.\n186\t2. **Validate the cheap version first:** put `mg jdi` + ntfy on a VPS and run a\n187\t   real job from a phone. The away-digest experience may cover a large share of\n188\t   the \"control from anywhere\" need at near-zero cost, and it proves the server\n189\t   story before any web code exists.\n190\t3. **Sequence against the roadmap:** do #4 (headless — queue + away digest) and\n191\t   #5 (event-streaming) first, then:\n192\t   - **Job one:** ~~the daemon — `mg serve`, project registry, bearer-token auth,\n193\t     read-only API first (projects, jobs, files, jdi status), embedded static UI\n194\t     later~~ — **DONE as the listener job** (see the supersession note above;\n195\t     the embedded UI is job three).\n196\t   - **Job two:** the mutating API + background run supervision with streamed\n197\t     logs + WebSocket/SSE.\n198\t   - **Job three:** the Svelte app — a web port of the TUI's design, embedded\n199\t     into the Go binary so the deployment story stays \"one binary on a server\"\n200\t     (very on-brand for a project that reduced itself to exactly one Go binary\n201\t     on purpose).\n202\t4. **Add `mg pull` as a small standalone job** — needed by the server story,\n203\t   useful locally, small enough to land on its own.\n204\t\n205\tRight direction, right product fit, wrong size for one go. Narrow it to the\n206\tcontrol plane, sequence it behind the roadmap's #4/#5, and it becomes three\n207\tclean jobs instead of one unbounded bet."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"561e42e9-f91a-46b3-ab07-d4e54ed01e9c","uuid":"6bc29d85-fae7-4fa3-b2df-eb797e9ef3db","timestamp":"2026-08-29T12:59:58.692Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/web-interface.md","content":"# Web interface: running manigot on a server with a browser control plane\n\nExploration notes (2026-08-27). Verdict: **REVISIT** — the direction is right and\nthe product is already 70% server-shaped, but the scope as originally phrased\n(\"command everything… create jobs, delete, merge, pull, whatever\") is a vision,\nnot a buildable unit, and one fork decides everything. This document records the\nfindings, the fork, the scope, and the recommended path.\n\n## Decision recorded (2026-08-27)\n\nA sibling idea was evaluated the same day and rejected: **external ticketing\nintegration (Linear / Gitea)** — syncing manigot's job state into a third-party\nissue tracker. It answers the *same* question this document answers (remote\nvisibility/control of jobs away from the terminal), and the web control plane\nreplaces it outright: it renders the real state (four files, diff, run logs)\nnatively and can act on it (launch, merge, delete), whereas a ticketing sync is\na lossy, view-only shadow that adds a second source of truth. It only re-enters\nconsideration as a later *consumer* of the event stream (post status/comments to\na team's board) if a team-need appears — never as a parallel control surface.\nThe one piece worth building independently of this decision is the git-forge\nstep (push job branch + open/merge PR on `mg done`) — the web UI needs it too,\nand it is small.\n\n## The idea\n\nRun manigot on an always-on server instead of a personal workstation, and replace\nthe TUI with a secured web interface that talks to a server listener to control\nit. A Svelte UI showing projects, their jobs, and job state — with the ability to\ncreate jobs, delete, merge, pull, launch agents, and watch runs — replacing the\nterminal-bound `mg tui`.\n\n## What already works on a server (the good news)\n\nThe heavy machinery is mostly terminal-independent already:\n\n- **`internal/job`** — `CreateJob`, `FinishJob`, `DeleteJob`, `Discover`,\n  `ReadJDIStatus` are pure functions over a project root. The TUI calls them\n  in-process; an HTTP handler can call the same functions in-process. No\n  subprocess mg, no TTY.\n- **`internal/git`** — worktree create/remove, squash merge, push, diff, commits.\n  All host-side, all callable.\n- **`mg jdi`** — already runs fully unattended via the `--print` path, writes a\n  pollable status sidecar (`.manigot/jdi-status/`), handles `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:`\n  markers, and pushes ntfy notifications. This is exactly the state model a web\n  UI would render — the TUI already polls it; the web UI would poll/stream the\n  same files.\n- **The sweep-commit** — every job-worktree session ends committed by the host.\n  This is what makes headless server operation safe in the first place.\n- **Docker isolation** — the agent containment story is the product's core\n  safety property and works identically on a server. The daemon does not touch\n  it.\n\nThe genuinely new pieces:\n\n1. **A long-running daemon** — today everything is a one-shot CLI that discovers\n   its project from `$PWD`. A server is a new process (`mg serve` or similar)\n   that holds a registry of project roots. **Multi-project is a new concept for\n   manigot** — nothing in the product today knows about more than one project\n   per invocation.\n2. **The HTTP API + auth surface** — today the trust boundary is the user's own\n   terminal; nothing authenticates anything. A server daemon is a remote attack\n   surface holding subscription credentials and docker access. This is the part\n   with real teeth (see Security).\n3. **Background run supervision with streamed output** — today's interactive\n   agent launches spawn terminal windows/tmux panes, which is meaningless on a\n   server. Web-launched agents need to be detached processes with captured logs\n   the UI can watch live.\n4. **The Svelte app itself.**\n5. **\"Pull\"** — there is no host-side fetch/pull in manigot today (only\n   push-to-origin and merge-default-branch). For a server, `mg pull` (fetch +\n   fast-forward the base branch, refuse-or-report on divergence rather than\n   merging blindly) is a small, genuinely useful addition — locally too.\n\n## The fork that decides everything\n\n**Is the web UI a control plane for background runs, or a remote terminal for\ninteractive sessions?**\n\n- **Control plane (recommended for v1):** launch `mg jdi` per job, launch\n  one-shot agent runs via the `--print` path, watch their logs live, review the\n  four files, edit `brief.md`, merge (`done`), delete, push, answer\n  `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` markers. This is the product's own trajectory — the\n  autonomy machinery exists precisely so the human's job is *review and decide*,\n  not drive. It reuses everything above.\n- **Interactive browser terminals** (xterm.js + a PTY bridge so you can drop\n  into a live Claude Code chat from the browser): much heavier, and arguably\n  wrong for a server — the human usually isn't at the server, and an interactive\n  session on a machine other people can reach raises \"who is driving this\"\n  questions. The roadmap already has \"in-TUI embedded terminal\" as its biggest,\n  last bet; building the same bet on a second surface at the same time is how\n  scope explodes.\n\nDecide that fork first, and you've decided the size of the whole thing.\n\n## User perspective\n\nFor the person this actually serves — a developer who wants manigot's work\nrunning on a box that's always on, reachable from anywhere — the control-plane\nversion is a genuine unlock: create a job from the browser, fire jdi at it, get\nan ntfy ping when it needs a human, open the verdict from your phone, approve\nand merge. The *interactive* version is a different product aimed at a different\nneed (remote pair-driving an agent), and it's the expensive one.\n\nA caution on the \"sleek, dynamic Svelte interface\": the roadmap's current state\nis praised as \"boring in the right places,\" and that is a product property, not\nan accident. The web UI should be a **port of the TUI's information design** —\nid / status / stage / type / date / title, the four files, the diff tab, the\nsame actions — not a re-imagination of what a dashboard should be. \"Dynamic\"\nshould mean *live updates*, which comes from the event stream, not from adding\n40 controls. If the interface exists at all, it must be as disciplined as the\nTUI.\n\n## Scope assessment\n\nWhat's missing before this is a brief:\n\n- **Single user or team?** One bearer token vs. real accounts. This changes the\n  auth design completely. (Recommendation: single-user/small-team token auth is\n  the right v1; user accounts are a different product.)\n- **Internet-exposed or LAN/VPN?** Determines how much hardening is\n  non-negotiable vs. nice-to-have.\n- **Project registry:** where do the projects come from? A config file of roots\n  is the obvious v1 (a scan-dir is a worse idea — you don't want a daemon\n  auto-adopting repos it finds).\n- **\"Pull\" semantics** need pinning down (fetch + fast-forward, report-don't-\n  merge on divergence, refuse on dirty tree).\n- **Sequencing against the roadmap.** This is the one that matters most: the web\n  UI's core value depends on roadmap items **#4 (headless/cron — queue,\n  non-terminal trigger, away digest)** and **#5 (event-streaming)**. The\n  \"sleek, dynamic\" part *is* the event stream made visible; polling the coarse\n  sidecars is exactly the non-dynamic experience to avoid. The roadmap's own\n  caution applies here as much as it did for the TUI: don't let the web UI's\n  requirements design the event format. So the honest path is: **#4 → #5 → web\n  UI**, with the web UI as the event stream's real consumer — which is a *better*\n  first consumer than the richer run.log the roadmap currently names.\n\n## Security\n\nThe daemon is a new trust boundary with root-adjacent power: it drives docker\nand git and holds subscription credentials. A compromised HTTP endpoint means an\nattacker running agents on the paid subscription and committing to the repos.\nNon-negotiable hardening:\n\n- **TLS** — in-binary or, saner, behind a reverse proxy (Caddy/nginx).\n- **Bearer-token auth** — over TLS only.\n- **Zero path inputs from the client** — every operation resolved against the\n  registered project roots; no job IDs or paths taken from the URL and trusted.\n- **Credentials never returned** by any API or rendered in the UI. The `.env`\n  holds subscription credentials; the daemon must never expose them.\n- **Audit trail** — a remote/shared box changes the accountability picture; the\n  local TUI never needed \"who ran what,\" the server does.\n- **Concurrency** — today every operation is naturally serialized (one CLI\n  process, user-driven). A daemon gets simultaneous web requests plus\n  long-running launches. `internal/git` has no locking; two concurrent\n  `done`/`delete` on the same job would race. The daemon needs per-project (or\n  per-job) serialization of mutating operations.\n- **Cost** — a server makes firing runs easy, and unattended agent runs burn\n  subscription quota. Surface run activity in the UI; keep ntfy as the \"away\n  digest\" rather than building new notification machinery.\n\n## Concerns\n\n1. **The daemon's power** — see Security. This is the part with real teeth.\n2. **Concurrency** — no locking in `internal/git`; the daemon must serialize\n   mutating operations per job.\n3. **Cost** — unattended runs on a server burn subscription quota.\n4. **Scope creep** — \"and pull, whatever\" is how a control-plane job becomes a\n   remote-desktop job. The interactive-terminal slice must be explicitly parked\n   (it's the roadmap's item #6 bet, one surface at a time).\n\n## Recommendation\n\n> **Superseded (2026-08-27):** the \"Job one: the daemon\" slice below — `mg serve`\n> + project registry + bearer-token auth + read-only API — is now **DONE**, as\n> the listener job (see\n> [`docs/jobs/wood_listener-architecture/brief.md`](jobs/wood_listener-architecture/brief.md),\n> which resolves the web-vs-GUI fork and supersedes this recommendation; no need\n> to re-litigate). This document remains the record of the exploration and the\n> later jobs (mutating API + run supervision, then one frontend) still sequence\n> as described.\n\nProceed, in this order:\n\n1. **Decide the fork** — control plane for background runs, no interactive\n   browser terminals in v1. Stake the product on that.\n2. **Validate the cheap version first:** put `mg jdi` + ntfy on a VPS and run a\n   real job from a phone. The away-digest experience may cover a large share of\n   the \"control from anywhere\" need at near-zero cost, and it proves the server\n   story before any web code exists.\n3. **Sequence against the roadmap:** do #4 (headless — queue + away digest) and\n   #5 (event-streaming) first, then:\n   - **Job one:** ~~the daemon — `mg serve`, project registry, bearer-token auth,\n     read-only API first (projects, jobs, files, jdi status), embedded static UI\n     later~~ — **DONE as the listener job** (see the supersession note above;\n     the embedded UI is job three).\n   - **Job two:** the mutating API + background run supervision with streamed\n     logs + WebSocket/SSE.\n   - **Job three:** the Svelte app — a web port of the TUI's design, embedded\n     into the Go binary so the deployment story stays \"one binary on a server\"\n     (very on-brand for a project that reduced itself to exactly one Go binary\n     on purpose).\n4. **Add `mg pull` as a small standalone job** — needed by the server story,\n   useful locally, small enough to land on its own.\n\nRight direction, right product fit, wrong size for one go. Narrow it to the\ncontrol plane, sequence it behind the roadmap's #4/#5, and it becomes three\nclean jobs instead of one unbounded bet.","numLines":207,"startLine":1,"totalLines":207}}}
