{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VwVgQcThmqr3YLgYMChMgY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tpackage main\n2\t\n3\timport (\n4\t\t\"bytes\"\n5\t\t\"fmt\"\n6\t\t\"io\"\n7\t\t\"os\"\n8\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n9\t\t\"strings\"\n10\t\t\"time\"\n11\t\n12\t\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/job\"\n13\t\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/orchestrate\"\n14\t)\n15\t\n16\t// sidecarExcludePattern is the .gitignore-syntax line ensureSidecarIgnored\n17\t// adds to a project's .git/info/exclude — every job's status sidecar, not\n18\t// just one, since the pattern is directory-shaped\n19\t// (\".manigot/jdi-status/\"). Rebuilt from the same constants\n20\t// (job.ManigotRelDir + job.JDISidecarDirName) the Go path construction\n21\t// uses, so the exclude pattern can never drift from where the sidecar is\n22\t// actually written.\n23\tvar sidecarExcludePattern = filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Join(job.ManigotRelDir, job.JDISidecarDirName)) + \"/\"\n24\t\n25\t// ensureSidecarIgnored appends sidecarExcludePattern to root's\n26\t// .git/info/exclude if not already present.\n27\t//\n28\t// This matters because the status/run.log sidecar lives inside\n29\t// the *target project's* .manigot/ — not manigot's own checkout — and the\n30\t// whole point of keeping it outside every job's own directory is so it can\n31\t// never be swept into an agent's own `git add -A`. That only actually holds\n32\t// if *this project's* git configuration excludes it: manigot's own\n33\t// .gitignore has no bearing on a project mg-jdi is driving. Rather than\n34\t// assume (or silently mutate) the project's own tracked .gitignore,\n35\t// .git/info/exclude is the right mechanism — local-only, per-checkout, never\n36\t// itself committed — so mg-jdi can guarantee this without touching anything\n37\t// the project's own history would ever see. Idempotent: safe to call on\n38\t// every mg-jdi run.\n39\t//\n40\t// Best-effort: a failure here (root isn't a git repo,